Standard Exercism Forth problem. Key points:

- `evaluate(input_data)` takes a list of lines and returns the stack (a list of integers, bottom to top).
- Numbers: a sequence of ASCII digits. Note that in the canonical tests, `-1` is a negative number — the spec says "a number is a sequence of one or more (ASCII) digits", but the tests push `-1` onto the stack. The standard Exercism solution handles `-` as part of a number. In fact, the canonical data includes negative numbers, and the reference implementation treats `-NNN` as a number. The Python reference solution uses the regex `^(-?\d+)$` for numbers.
- Words are case-insensitive.
- User-defined words: `: name definition ;`. The definition is stored as a token list (already parsed). Important test: "can define a word that uses a word with the same name" — `: foo 10 ;` followed by `: foo foo 1 + ;` — the inner `foo` must refer to the *old* definition. So the definition should be captured at the time of definition. If we store the definition as a list of tokens and resolve words at execution time, then when `foo` calls `foo`, it would recurse infinitely. So the standard approach is to store the definition as a list of tokens and resolve each token to a callable at definition time. In the reference solution, each word maps to a list of callables resolved at definition time.

